      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 by The Journal of Bone and Joint Surgery, Incorporated.",
        "AbstractText": "Scapular winging is a rare, underreported, and debilitating disorder that produces abnormal scapulothoracic kinematics, which can lead to shoulder weakness, decreased range of motion, and substantial pain. Although there are numerous underlying etiologies, injuries to the long thoracic nerve or spinal accessory nerve are the most common, with resultant neuromuscular imbalance in the scapulothoracic stabilizing muscles. Early diagnosis followed by initiation of a treatment algorithm is important for successful outcomes. Most cases resolve with nonsurgical management. However, in patients with persistent symptoms despite nonsurgical management, appropriate dynamic muscle transfers can effectively treat the scapular winging, with good clinical outcomes."
      },
